# AWS credentials

Nothing in this repo reads AWS credentials directly — `envie` shells out to
`terraform` and `aws`, which use the standard credential chain. Get credentials
by wrapping the command in `aws-vault`:

```bash
aws-vault exec personal --no-session -- cargo run -- deploy --env test-123
aws-vault exec personal --no-session -- terraform plan
aws-vault exec personal --no-session -- aws sts get-caller-identity
```

`--no-session` is required: the `personal` profile's cached STS session token is
usually expired, and `--no-session` skips `sts:GetSessionToken` so long-lived
credentials are used directly.

For a whole shell session of AWS work, use `aws-vault exec personal --no-session
-- zsh` once instead of prefixing every command.

## Cost discipline when testing

Only create resources that are free or effectively free, and always tear them
down in the same session:

- Safe: SSM parameters (Standard tier — the best choice for end-to-end tests, as
  they are free, instant to create and have real outputs to pass between units),
  S3 buckets (empty), DynamoDB tables on-demand, IAM roles/policies, Lambda (free
  tier), API Gateway, CloudWatch log groups, SQS, SNS, Step Functions (standard).
- Avoid unless the user explicitly asks: NAT gateways, RDS, EKS, ElastiCache,
  Redshift, Fargate/EC2 instances, Global Accelerator, anything with an hourly
  charge.
- Prefer `terraform plan` / `envie deploy --dry-run` over `apply` when the goal
  is only to validate generated configuration.
- After an `apply`, always `destroy`, and delete any bootstrap S3 bucket and
  DynamoDB lock table the test created.

Use `eu-west-1` unless a test needs otherwise, and prefix test resource names
with `envie-test-` so leftovers are identifiable.
